  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126057,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 137,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients typically present with a papule with a central keratotic plug.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:28:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126058,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 137,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>Pilar sheath acanthoma is a symmetrical, well-circumscribed, vertically oriented lesion. It consists of a large central crater filled by basket-weave orthokeratotic keratin and formed as a result of the confluence of several contiguous dilated follicular infundibula. Emanating from this central crater, in a radial arrangement, are solid lobules composed of pale eosinophilic cells similar to those seen in the outer root sheath of the isthmus of the hair follicle. These lobules often show peripheral palisading of the cells and contain small ducts lined by a wavy eosinophilic cuticle. In rare instances, the neoplastic lobules are distributed in a horizontal configuration, and they may contain ductal structures showing decapitation secretion or contain small clusters of mature sebocytes {28609346; 7235505}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>This includes dilated pore of Winer and trichofolliculoma.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:28:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
